WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:05.140
Speaker 1: Let's start today with a sort of
thick and meaty policy segment.

00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:08.880
And let's talk about Social Security in the
context of this.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:15.379
And it's been much requested by many of you
is the absolute apoplexy from Republicans

00:00:15.379 --> 00:00:19.789
at the mere suggestion that over the last
few years they've hinted at and even sometimes

00:00:19.789 --> 00:00:25.470
overtly said maybe we should cut Social Security,
maybe the cuts would be lower benefits, maybe

00:00:25.470 --> 00:00:29.109
the cuts would be you have to work longer,
meaning raise the retirement age or whatever

00:00:29.109 --> 00:00:30.449
the case may be.

00:00:30.449 --> 00:00:34.701
But when Joe Biden said at his State of the
Union address a couple of weeks ago, some

00:00:34.701 --> 00:00:37.350
of these Republicans want to cut Social Security.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:43.530
They went crazy and started saying Joe Biden
is a liar and we would never do that.

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:45.420
And on and on and on.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:54.239
And then yesterday on CNBC incomes former
Vice President Mike Pence, and he says, oh,

00:00:54.239 --> 00:00:58.840
we should definitely put on the table that
we might cut Social Security and Medicare.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:02.129
They've spent weeks saying we would never
do that.

00:01:02.129 --> 00:01:08.710
Stop accusing us of maybe wanting to be open
to considering the possibility of ever cutting

00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:10.540
Social Security and Medicare.

00:01:10.540 --> 00:01:13.672
And then here's Mike Pence saying, oh, yeah,
no, like we should definitely think of that

00:01:13.672 --> 00:01:14.672
because of the debt, is.

00:01:14.672 --> 00:01:20.490
Speaker 2: That while I respect the speaker's
commitment to take Social Security and Medicare

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:25.860
off the table for the debt ceiling negotiations,
we've got to put them on the table in the

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:26.930
long term.

00:01:26.930 --> 00:01:30.720
And right now, President Biden's policy is
insolvency.

00:01:30.720 --> 00:01:38.060
I mean, we're looking at a debt crisis in
this country over the next 25 years that is

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:39.220
driven by entitlements.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:43.390
And nobody in Washington, D.C., wants to talk
about it.

00:01:43.390 --> 00:01:45.341
I've been going around the country.

00:01:45.341 --> 00:01:46.869
I'm going to continue to try and do my part.

00:01:46.869 --> 00:01:48.180
Yeah, people, what.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:50.479
Speaker 1: A heroic thing he's doing.

00:01:50.479 --> 00:01:51.830
What would you do about entitlements?

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:55.079
Speaker 2: Well, I think first and foremost,
you have to explain to people what's going

00:01:55.079 --> 00:01:56.079
on.

00:01:56.079 --> 00:02:00.320
We've got a $32 trillion national debt right
now, Andrew.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:05.710
Having a national debt the same size as our
nation's economy is unsustainable.

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:06.880
Last time that happened was right after.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:07.880
Oh, I don't disagree with you.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.229
I'm just curious what's on the for you on
entitlements?

00:02:10.229 --> 00:02:11.229
What's on the table?

00:02:11.229 --> 00:02:13.599
Well, I think it begins with education.

00:02:13.599 --> 00:02:15.560
It begins with having the American people
understand.

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:16.560
Yeah.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.790
Speaker 1: So twice, like what exactly would
you do?

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:21.019
Well, we got to tell people all about it.

00:02:21.019 --> 00:02:28.099
Remember what happened at the State of the
Union when Joe Biden merely suggested some

00:02:28.099 --> 00:02:31.120
Republicans are open to cutting.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:32.890
Speaker 3: Take the economy hostage?

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:33.890
I get it.

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:37.010
Unless I agree to the record, I can't.

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:39.550
All of you at home should know what those
plans are now.

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:43.190
Instead of making the wealthy pay their fair
share.

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:47.500
Some Republicans, some Republicans want Medicare
and Social Security sunset.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.660
I'm not saying that a majority.

00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:50.660
Unidentified: No.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:53.980
Let me give you anybody who doesn't contact
my office.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:55.780
I'll give you a copy.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:03:01.250
Speaker 3: I'll give you a copy of the proposal.

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:05.799
Speaker 1: And there was you some Marjorie
Trader GREENE standing and screaming.

00:03:05.799 --> 00:03:08.620
So they say for weeks and weeks.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:12.569
No, we would never, ever, ever, ever, ever
do that.

00:03:12.569 --> 00:03:13.569
And then here goes Mike Pence.

00:03:13.569 --> 00:03:15.489
And he's like, Oh, yeah, it should absolutely
be on the table.

00:03:15.489 --> 00:03:16.629
We have way too much debt.

00:03:16.629 --> 00:03:21.350
So let's talk about the truth on this talking
point so that we can all understand what is

00:03:21.350 --> 00:03:25.000
going on, because the right says Social Security
is insolvent.

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:27.209
It just doesn't work financially.

00:03:27.209 --> 00:03:28.349
It doesn't work.

00:03:28.349 --> 00:03:29.349
Here's the truth.

00:03:29.349 --> 00:03:34.959
And the truth is it's it's a good thing and
a bad thing that it's really clear what's

00:03:34.959 --> 00:03:36.060
going on.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:39.260
Because sometimes when things are so clear
and simple, the right loves to pretend like

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:41.020
they're really complicated.

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:45.959
Despite Republicans claiming Social Security
is insolvent, meaning unable to pay the benefits

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:48.580
that it owes or will owe people.

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:54.250
If we do nothing, we change nothing at all
about Social Security.

00:03:54.250 --> 00:03:59.579
There is enough money to pay full benefits
until 2035.

00:03:59.579 --> 00:04:03.260
That means, like if you keep doing just the
inflation adjustments for cost of living to

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:07.970
what people get, you change nothing about
what people in other than continuing to do

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:14.409
the inflation adjustment to the Social Security
cap like literally nothing changes until 2048,

00:04:14.409 --> 00:04:15.409
2035.

00:04:15.409 --> 00:04:17.220
There is no problem.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:23.340
And if we continue with the do nothing scenario
starting in 2035, you can reduce benefits

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:30.250
to 80% of what they would be inflation adjusted
and then it still continues to be fine, making

00:04:30.250 --> 00:04:34.539
no changes whatsoever other than moving benefits
from 180%.

00:04:34.539 --> 00:04:38.070
Now, what is the reason that this is going
on?

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:41.580
Why is this even a conversation changing demographics.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:45.560
More and more people are retiring relative
to the number of workers.

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:49.980
So as baby boomers continue to retire, the
number of people receiving benefits relative

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:55.450
to the number of people paying in is going
to fall out of balance towards the retirees.

00:04:55.450 --> 00:05:00.080
And then secondly, people on average are living
longer, meaning that the estimates for how

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:04.650
long people are going to be receiving benefits,
it's more years because people are living

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:05.650
longer.

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:06.650
Okay.

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:10.180
Now, I think it's unacceptable to go down
to 80% of benefits.

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:11.880
But again.

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:13.979
With doing nothing.

00:05:13.979 --> 00:05:19.389
Fine until 2035 and then fine for a very long
time on 80% of benefits.

00:05:19.389 --> 00:05:23.830
I believe if you've paid in based on getting
100% of benefits, you should get them.

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:24.830
Okay.

00:05:24.830 --> 00:05:25.870
But I've laid out the do nothing scenario.

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:28.129
It's not exactly insolvent, is it?

00:05:28.129 --> 00:05:33.620
It's everything's fine for 11, 12 years, and
then we do a 20% reduction.

00:05:33.620 --> 00:05:34.860
But let's fix it.

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:35.860
Okay.

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:36.860
How do we fix it?

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:37.860
There's quite a few ways.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:39.550
They're relatively simple.

00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:42.810
It's just a matter of finding the political
will to do it.

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:50.229
As a reminder, right now, in 2023, Social
Security contributions are made on your first

00:05:50.229 --> 00:05:54.419
$160,000 that you earn per year.

00:05:54.419 --> 00:05:59.150
Once you get beyond 160, you stop paying into
Social Security.

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:02.220
The amount that is paid in is 12.4%.

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:07.030
Now, many of you might say, David, I'm only
paying 6.2%.

00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:14.900
The reason is that in a normal job, if you're
a W-2 employee, that 12.4% is divided between

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:15.930
you and your employer.

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:19.750
So instead of you paying 12.4%, you pay 6.2.

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:21.259
Your employer pays 6.2.

00:06:21.259 --> 00:06:24.860
If you're self-employed like me, you pay the
full 12.4%.

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:26.979
Okay, so what can you do?

00:06:26.979 --> 00:06:29.110
One thing you can do is you can increase that
rate.

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:34.110
You could say instead of 12.4%, it's going
to be something higher on that first hundred

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:35.669
and $60,000.

00:06:35.669 --> 00:06:42.039
I don't like that idea because it disproportionately
hits those earning under 160,000.

00:06:42.039 --> 00:06:43.039
But you could do that.

00:06:43.039 --> 00:06:47.289
You could sense that a 12.4% were going to
pay 15%, and that's going to stretch out how

00:06:47.289 --> 00:06:49.550
long we can pay 100% of benefits.

00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:54.979
Second option, you can raise the cap so you
keep it at 12.4%.

00:06:54.979 --> 00:07:00.199
But instead of paying that on your first hundred
and 60,000 that you make, maybe you paid on

00:07:00.199 --> 00:07:03.639
your first 180 or maybe on your first 200.

00:07:03.639 --> 00:07:06.509
That seems like a better option to me.

00:07:06.509 --> 00:07:09.759
Number three, you could raise the retirement
age.

00:07:09.759 --> 00:07:11.080
It's very controversial.

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:15.610
I don't like it, but you could say, well,
you've got to wait longer before you can get

00:07:15.610 --> 00:07:16.610
benefits.

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:22.370
What I do like is giving a bonus if you delay
retirement by choice.

00:07:22.370 --> 00:07:24.210
This is already part of the way it works.

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:29.610
And for people for whom this is new, I think
it's useful to know this stuff right now.

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:33.240
You can start receiving reduced benefits at
age 62.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:36.860
The standard age to get benefits is 67.

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:40.960
So your 100% benefit would be if you say at
67 years of age.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:44.639
I want to start getting the benefits from
62 to 67.

00:07:44.639 --> 00:07:46.210
You get less.

00:07:46.210 --> 00:07:50.879
And if you at age 67 say, I don't need the
money yet either because I have private retirement

00:07:50.879 --> 00:07:52.930
or I'm going to continue working or whatever.

00:07:52.930 --> 00:07:58.300
If you say I'm going to wait until I'm 68
or 69 or 70, you get even more benefits when

00:07:58.300 --> 00:07:59.860
you do retire.

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:02.319
We could expand that and say, Hey, you know
what?

00:08:02.319 --> 00:08:07.800
People are living longer and many people would
gladly delay getting their Social Security

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:08.800
benefits.

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:15.990
You could say if you choose to wait until
age 71 or 72 or 73, 74, you get more benefits,

00:08:15.990 --> 00:08:18.129
which leaves money in the trust fund.

00:08:18.129 --> 00:08:20.360
Then it can accrue earnings and gains.

00:08:20.360 --> 00:08:23.479
You don't force anybody to do anything, but
you give an option.

00:08:23.479 --> 00:08:24.690
That's interesting.

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:31.220
A fourth option is to move the money in the
Social Security trust fund to something with

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:34.390
potentially higher return, but with more risk.

00:08:34.390 --> 00:08:39.070
Right now, Social Security invests in low
risk government bonds.

00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:41.130
Very safe, very low return.

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:43.820
Without going into it, we're going to buy
Bitcoin.

00:08:43.820 --> 00:08:48.960
You could slightly expand the list of investment
vehicles that Social Security trust fund can

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:53.270
be invested in with the idea that there will
be a little more volatility.

00:08:53.270 --> 00:08:58.180
But over time you should get a higher return
and that could close that gap that will eventually

00:08:58.180 --> 00:08:59.190
appear.

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:04.980
And then lastly, you could also increase immigration,
bring in people to work to pay into the system

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:07.560
in order to even out that ratio.

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:10.209
So we can fix this with some tweaks.

00:09:10.209 --> 00:09:14.450
You could raise the cap a little bit and then
you could offer greater benefits to people

00:09:14.450 --> 00:09:17.620
who choose to delay retirement a little more.

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:21.350
Increase immigration a little bit, to increase
the number of people paying in and consider

00:09:21.350 --> 00:09:26.670
slightly higher yield but slightly higher
risk investments with some of the money.

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:32.790
It's not brain surgery, but it's a political
gridlock and we have the solutions.

00:09:32.790 --> 00:09:36.709
If only we could get our elected officials
to put them in place.

00:09:36.709 --> 00:09:43.529
Lastly, mixing this specific issue of the
Social Security trust fund generally with

00:09:43.529 --> 00:09:48.880
that of the national debt is also very dishonest
because the truth is Social Security is its

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:52.660
own trust fund and we can manage and make
tweaks to that individually.

00:09:52.660 --> 00:09:56.589
The conversation about the debt is fine, but
it really is a separate conversation.

00:09:56.589 --> 00:10:01.550
So after weeks of saying we would never do
that, Mike Pence shows up and says, Oh yeah,

00:10:01.550 --> 00:11:07.620
we should definitely consider that.

